In Ladybird, margins collapse through an empty block whether or
not it has clearance. Clearance goes above the top margin of the
block, and keeps it apart from the margins before the block. So the
flow goes on from where the margins of the block began, collapses
them with the margins after it, and keeps the resulting margin
inside the parent of the block.

Blink’s BlockLayoutAlgorithm::FinishInflow() notes such a child in
self_collapsing_child_had_clearance, and lays it out again with a margin
strut that holds only its own top margin. ComputeInflowPosition() then
moves the flow past the margin before the clearance and the clearance
itself, and FinishLayout() adds the end margin strut to the size of
the parent. WebKit’s RenderBlockFlow::clearFloatsIfNeeded() resets
the margins it tracks to those of a child that isSelfCollapsingBlock()
holds for, sets the logical height back to the top margin of the
child, and keeps the margins from collapsing with the parent’s, with
setCanCollapseMarginAfterWithChildren(). Gecko’s nsBlockReflowContext::
PlaceBlock() includes the top margin of an empty block in its bottom
margin, and backs up the advance of its containing block by that margin.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5, the test’s 26
cases come out the same. After two such blocks in a row, WebKit puts
what follows 20px higher than Chrome, Firefox and Ladybird do, so
the test leaves that out.
